From d618778fe306806ef1f5ad24f0c4b4802dc9f522 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:42:19 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] airoha: add support for Gemtek XR1710G

Gemtek XR1710G, sold by Brightspeed, is an AN7581 Wi-Fi 7 gateway.
It is a close relative of the Gemtek W1700K: the Silabs
Bluetooth/Zigbee radio and the Airoha GPS are gone, and the LEDs of
the two 1 GbE ports are driven by the internal switch PHYs instead of
GPIOs.

Specification:

- SoC       : Airoha AN7581GT, 4x Cortex-A53
- RAM       : 2 GiB DDR4-2666 (ESMT M16U8G16512A)
- Flash     : 512 MiB SPI-NAND (Winbond W25N04KV)
- WLAN      : MediaTek MT7996 (BE19000) on PCIe
  - 2.4 GHz : 4x4, MT7976GN
  - 5 GHz   : 4x4, MT7977BN
  - 6 GHz   : 4x4, MT7977AN
- Ethernet  : 2x 10 GbE (Realtek RTL8261BE, wan and lan2),
              2x 1 GbE (AN7581 internal switch, lan3 and lan4)
- LEDs      : 1x status, red/green/blue/white (GPIO),
              2x2 lan3/lan4 green/amber (switch PHY)
- Button    : reset
- Fan       : PWM controlled by a Nuvoton NCT7802 on I2C
- UART      : 5-pin header next to the case screw, 115200n8
  - pinout  : TX, GND, VCC, N/C, RX, starting at the screw
- Power     : 12 VDC, 5 A, barrel jack, center positive
- Bootloader: U-Boot 2014.04-rc1 "AXON 1.6", signed, vendor BMT/BBT

The vendor bootloader verifies the image it boots by default and uses
Airoha BMT/BBT for bad block management, which Linux does not know
about. As on the W1700K, a U-Boot chainloader is therefore placed into
the "chainloader" partition (the start of the first vendor firmware
bank), which the vendor bootloader writes and loads, and everything
else lives in UBI, which handles bad blocks itself. The vendor
bootloader and its 66 MiB BMT pool at the end of flash are left alone.

Installation (via serial console):

1. Open the case: there is a Torx T10 screw under the QR code label,
   then pry the grey and white halves apart starting at the ports.
2. Connect to the UART header and interrupt the vendor bootloader with
   any key.
3. Make the vendor bootloader boot the chainloader from flash, and
   write the chainloader with TFTP (server 192.168.1.254). The vendor
   Ethernet driver is flaky, the tftpboot may need a few attempts:

     setenv serverip 192.168.1.254
     setenv ipaddr 192.168.1.1
     tftpboot 0x89000000 openwrt-airoha-an7581-gemtek_xr1710g-ubi-chainload-uboot.itb
     setenv one flash read 0x600000 0x100000 \$loadaddr
     setenv two "; bootm"
     setenv bootcmd "$one$two"
     saveenv
     flash erase 0x600000 0x100000
     flash write 0x600000 0x100000 0x89000000
     reset

4. The device reboots into the chainloader menu. Boot the UBI installer
   from https://github.com/hurrian/w1700k-ubi-installer with "Boot
   installer via TFTP". It creates the UBI volumes, copies the Wi-Fi
   calibration data and MAC addresses from the vendor partitions into
   the "factory" volume and installs OpenWrt.
5. Further updates use the sysupgrade.itb image.

Reverting to the stock firmware is not documented: the stock firmware
needs an ISP account to be useful.

MAC addresses ("factory" UBI volume):

- WAN     : 0x5000           xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
- LAN     : 0x6000           xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
- 2.4 GHz : 0x6000 + 1
- 5 GHz   : 0x6000 + 2
- 6 GHz   : 0x6000 + 3

Known issues:

- The 10 GbE ports (wan and lan2) may come up without link or lose
  link: the AN7581 USXGMII PCS sometimes fails RX calibration ("No
  FBCK Lock") with the RTL8261BE. The PCS and PHY fixes are being
  worked on separately.

Based on the XR1710G support by Kenneth Kasilag (openwrt/openwrt#22397).

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
